Ladybird advances each tab of a run from where the tab before it ends,
and takes the stop after the next one where the next is less than 0.5ch
away. WebKit’s FontCascade::tabWidth() and Blink’s Font::TabWidth()
compute each tab’s width from that tab’s own position, and so does
Gecko’s AdvanceToNextTab(), with a minimum advance of 0.5ch from
GetMinTabAdvanceAppUnits(). WebKit and Gecko take 0.5ch as the
threshold, as the spec does; Blink takes half the advance of a space.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, two tabs
after no text, after one character, and after seven characters all
end at 16 spaces (with tab-size: 8), and three tabs at 24. Under
tab-size: 2px, three tabs end at 8px in Chrome, 18px in Firefox,
and 12px in WebKit; Ladybird gives 12px, as WebKit does, and as the
spec’s rule for each tab gives.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, a range around
each tab of a run spans from where the character before the tab ends to
the tab’s own stop — as “a\t\tX” under tab-size: 8 gives its tabs 1 to
8 and 8 to 16 spaces. Ladybird gives each tab a spacer glyph of its own,
so its ranges come out the same.
